course: dictionary
lesson: 7465
title: Porcelain and china
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words can name a material or a thing made from it. Read the words around porcelain to find out which it is. «China also gave the world porcelain, tea, kites and an early earthquake detector» lists things people make and use, so porcelain is something you can hold.
explain: «...China wanted silver and paid for it with silk, tea and porcelain.» Paying tells you porcelain is something people sell and buy. Now try the swap test: put "a hard, shiny clay made by baking" in place of porcelain. If the sentence still makes sense, you have the right meaning.
explain: Porcelain is also called china, and that is why the word china can mean both a country and cups and plates. Porcelain was invented in China, so people named it after that country. A word's history can help you remember what it means.
fact: Porcelain is a hard, white, shiny material made by baking fine clay at very high heat.
fact: Porcelain is also called china, because it was invented in China.
fact: Porcelain does not conduct electricity, so it is used in insulators.
fact: A «porcelain doll» is delicate and easily broken.
fact: Porcelain can also describe skin that is very pale and smooth.
exercise: What is porcelain made from? | Melted glass poured into moulds | Sand pressed together by hand | *Fine clay baked at very high heat | Wood cut and dried in the sun
exercise: Why is porcelain also called china? | Because China is a small island | *Because it was invented in China | Because it is the colour of tea
exercise: Porcelain does not conduct electricity. What is it useful for? | Lightbulb filaments | *Insulators | Batteries | Wires that carry electricity
test: What does a «porcelain doll» suggest? | Loud and strong | Waterproof and bendy | *Delicate and easily broken | Made of wood and paint
test: «China... paid for it with silk, tea and porcelain.» What does this suggest about porcelain? | Porcelain was a kind of silk | *China sold porcelain to pay for silver | Porcelain was made of silver | China never traded porcelain
exam: «Rare Ming blue-and-white porcelain can sell for millions, but ordinary pieces are not so valuable.» What does this tell you? | *Some porcelain is rare and worth a lot, but most is not | Ming porcelain is always cheap | All porcelain is worth millions | Porcelain has no value at all
